# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Impact is meaningful (unauthorized object read) but bounded to a short TOCTOU window and requires concurrent policy updates and favorable ACL conditions. This keeps severity at medium rather than high/critical despite remote reachability.
## Likelihood
high - Exploitation requires racing a narrow window during policy updates and depends on ACLs or prior access; an attacker can spam reads to increase the chance but timing is still constrained. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - During the stale-cache window, reads can succeed even though a newly added bucket policy should deny them, exposing object data to unauthorized access.
## Assumptions
- Deployment exposes the HTTP listener to potential attackers (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR may be non-loopback or behind a reverse proxy).
- A bucket policy is newly added to deny reads that were previously allowed by ACLs (e.g., public-read) or by existing credentials.
- An attacker can send GetObject/HeadObject/GetObjectAttributes requests during the short window after policy write but before fast-path cache update.
- No additional external controls (e.g., network allowlists) prevent the attacker from reaching the service.
- Bucket exists with object data
- Bucket policy is newly added or changed to deny reads
- Fast-path cache still indicates bucket_policy_present=false
- Attacker can issue object read requests during the TOCTOU window
- ACLs or existing credentials would otherwise allow read
## Path
[n1] GetObject/HeadObject -> [n2] load_bucket_handle_for_object_policy_read -> [n3] fast-path says no policy -> [n4] ACL-only decision -> [n5] object read allowed
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:1663-1704` - Early return when fast-path cache indicates bucket_policy_present=false, skipping policy load for object reads.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:733-760` - cached_bucket_policy_for_loaded_handle returns None when bucket_policy_present is false, preventing policy evaluation.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:4517-4538` - authorize_object_read relies on load_bucket_handle_for_object_policy_read for object read authorization.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket.rs:764-789` - put_bucket_policy writes policy to storage before updating the fast-path cache, creating a window for stale cache reads.
## Narrative
Object reads use load_bucket_handle_for_object_policy_read, which returns early when the fast-path cache says bucket_policy_present=false, skipping policy loading. cached_bucket_policy_for_loaded_handle also returns None when bucket_policy_present is false, ensuring no policy is evaluated. put_bucket_policy writes the policy to storage and only then updates the fast-path cache, creating a TOCTOU window where reads can observe a stale cache entry and be authorized by ACLs alone. The validation report describes a PoC test that forces a stale fast-path entry and observes a successful HeadObject despite a deny policy, indicating a real authorization bypass window. This is reachable through normal S3 object-read APIs when a policy is newly added or modified.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- Bucket ACLs and bucket policies
- Fast-path cache for bucket metadata
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests or runtime configuration were found; actual network exposure (public vs internal) is unknown.
- Static analysis only; the exact duration of the cache-staleness window and operational mitigations are not measurable here.
